From f3647c26ee4c05035619952f59e9e5340436b58d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 22:18:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: the second view is decoded where the display
 says that it takes 3D

videoplayer.hades3dmvc was a switch, on by default: every H.264 stream
with a second view (a Matroska file made of a 3D Blu-ray) went to the
decoder of two views, in software, and became a stereoscopic video, on
every machine. On a display that shows no 3D that gives nothing: the
video leaves the hardware decoder, Kodi asks how a stereoscopic video
is to be shown, and one eye is shown in the end.

The setting now has three values:

- automatic, the default: both views where the display says that it
  takes stereoscopic 3D. On any other display such a video stays the 2D
  video it always was, decoded as before;
- always: both views on any display, for anaglyph glasses or for a 3D
  display behind a device that hides what it is;
- off.

What the display says is asked of the window system
(CWinSystemBase::HasStereoDisplay(), false unless a window system knows
better). The GBM one finds it when Kodi starts on the display, as with
what the display says of HDR: the driver lists a mode with frame
packing, top-and-bottom or side-by-side (half) or, for a driver that
lists no such mode, the EDID has 3D in its HDMI vendor block
(STEREO::TakesStereo()). A line of the log says which.

The rule is KODI::MULTIVIEW::Allowed(), checked by the host test of
that header; TakesStereo() is checked by the host test of DRMStereo.h.
Neither has run in Kodi.
